{"parentUuid":null,"logicalParentUuid":"52d83e66-e2b4-451f-8cfb-dd968239a3aa","isSidechain":false,"type":"system","subtype":"compact_boundary","content":"Conversation compacted","level":"info","compactMetadata":{"trigger":"auto","preTokens":379560,"postTokens":29197,"cumulativeDroppedTokens":4178714,"durationMs":86499,"preCompactDiscoveredTools":["ArtifactComments","Monitor","SendMessage","WebFetch","WebSearch","mcp__ccd_session__start_session","mcp__ccd_session_mgmt__list_events","mcp__ccd_session_mgmt__search_session_transcripts","mcp__computer-use__app_ax_find","mcp__computer-use__app_batch","mcp__computer-use__app_bring_to_current_space","mcp__computer-use__app_click","mcp__computer-use__app_drag","mcp__computer-use__app_focus","mcp__computer-use__app_key","mcp__computer-use__app_list_windows","mcp__computer-use__app_menu","mcp__computer-use__app_release","mcp__computer-use__app_screenshot","mcp__computer-use__app_scroll","mcp__computer-use__app_type","mcp__computer-use__app_zoom","mcp__computer-use__computer_batch","mcp__computer-use__list_apps","mcp__computer-use__list_granted_applications","mcp__computer-use__open_application","mcp__computer-use__read_clipboard","mcp__computer-use__release_full_control","mcp__computer-use__request_access","mcp__computer-use__request_full_control","mcp__computer-use__request_teach_access","mcp__computer-use__switch_display","mcp__computer-use__teach_batch","mcp__computer-use__teach_step","mcp__computer-use__write_clipboard","mcp__playwright__browser_close","mcp__playwright__browser_emulate_media","mcp__playwright__browser_evaluate","mcp__playwright__browser_navigate","mcp__playwright__browser_resize","mcp__playwright__browser_take_screenshot"],"preCompactArtifactReadVersions":[{"slug":"6c3cbfd4-9305-484c-9fea-4f7fa7cfeeb3","ver":"1793745150-db9e"},{"slug":"e4519567-125b-4ce3-a8a6-d33f8afb3449","ver":"1793743042-9217"}],"preservedSegment":{"headUuid":"b45b9640-a0c4-4ebd-922e-14ffb3e7663a","anchorUuid":"1171f9e5-6efc-4afe-a3e9-175611c8c15f","tailUuid":"52d83e66-e2b4-451f-8cfb-dd968239a3aa"},"preservedMessages":{"anchorUuid":"1171f9e5-6efc-4afe-a3e9-175611c8c15f","uuids":["b45b9640-a0c4-4ebd-922e-14ffb3e7663a","e454adb4-151d-453a-837b-3efc0247fa6a","70eb3b97-e73b-4968-bb25-a922740247ae","c787b53a-83f9-4011-ab29-fe491b8276b6","9469c6fd-3b32-4c90-8e85-9c04b1f1b21f","15a33c09-9ba4-4399-ae87-1bf434e74250","52d83e66-e2b4-451f-8cfb-dd968239a3aa"],"allUuids":["b45b9640-a0c4-4ebd-922e-14ffb3e7663a","e454adb4-151d-453a-837b-3efc0247fa6a","70eb3b97-e73b-4968-bb25-a922740247ae","d59a04e9-497a-4254-a51a-1b138e8b7845","c787b53a-83f9-4011-ab29-fe491b8276b6","f65da5f7-3ecd-4935-bdfc-d4904cf96290","59f4b5cc-4639-452e-ba76-f274ba48cd21","bbff44d5-44c7-4b38-9b5b-e88c8c99421a","9469c6fd-3b32-4c90-8e85-9c04b1f1b21f","9f18f544-a039-4fe1-a609-2cd095bcdb0c","585e9c6d-4155-4f75-a6af-83009b733cee","b14d77c4-1311-4a5a-829a-ec68c1b545f2","15a33c09-9ba4-4399-ae87-1bf434e74250","52d83e66-e2b4-451f-8cfb-dd968239a3aa"]},"runningTurnSends":{"uuids":["c2795e20-5b41-458e-b0f6-074986df46c2"],"openerUuid":"c2795e20-5b41-458e-b0f6-074986df46c2"}},"uuid":"04c6d126-d16b-4886-b55e-83b959355af4","timestamp":"2026-11-05T23:38:39.648Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/kenneth/.claude/projects/-Users-kenneth-Projects-JusPrin--claude-worktrees-first-run-flow-f7be35","sessionId":"c868826d-3950-4f0f-9ecd-4a549cdd6983","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"claude/first-run-printer-step","slug":"dapper-knitting-moler"}
{"parentUuid":"04c6d126-d16b-4886-b55e-83b959355af4","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"instructions","files":[{"path":"/Users/kenneth/.claude/CLAUDE.md","type":"User","content":"# Global Claude Code Rules\n\n## How to Communicate\n\nTalk like a colleague explaining something out loud, not like a report.\n\n1. **Full sentences, one idea each.** Lead with a concrete case (someone opens a file, chats twenty times, then runs out of credits) before any general point. Never pack a mechanism into a noun phrase like \"hidden fair-use ceiling\". Say what the person sees happen, step by step.\n\n2. **No labels of your own.** Names you made up while working (\"phase 2\", \"board 6a\", \"the M4 batch\", \"the three decisions\") mean nothing to me. Name a thing by what it is or does. To ask about a screen, say where it is and send the picture.\n\n3. **Ask decisions in full, as questions.** Start with \"Decision needed\". Give one paragraph per decision: what it is, why it's mine, the options with what each costs, and your pick. End it with a \"?\". Never refer to a decision by a label, a count, or the report it's in. Don't hand me a choice you could settle yourself by searching or measuring.\n\n4. **Mark proposals as proposals.** Keep apart what the code does today, what you propose, and what I have agreed to. Never write an idea I haven't approved as if it were settled. Before calling something \"my rule\", find my actual words.\n\n5. **Report outcomes, not the journey.** Leave out problems you hit and solved unless they change what I do next. For an investigation, say what the problem was, how you solved it, what you tested and the result, and what's still open. Keep it under about 500 words.\n\n## Git Commit Policy\n\nWhen the user asks to commit changes:\n\n1. Run `git status` and `git diff` to review the changes.\n2. If you are confident that both the changes are correct and the proposed commit message accurately describes them, proceed with git commit without asking for confirmation.\n3. If you have any uncertainty about either, stop and ask the user for explicit confirmation before committing.\n\nWhen the user asks to revert or amend commits:\n1. Explain what will be reverted/amended\n2. Ask for confirmation before executing any `git revert`, `git reset`, or `git commit --amend` commands\n3. Only proceed after explicit user approval\n4. Never revert commits without explicit user confirmation.\n\nThis applies to all git operations that modify commit history.\n\n## Submodule Policy\n\n**Never commit or attempt to commit submodule updates.**\n\nWhen `git status` shows modified submodules (e.g., `modified: some-submodule (new commits)`):\n- Do not stage or commit the submodule change\n- Do not include submodules when running `git add`\n- Leave submodule management to the user\n\n## Testing Principles\n\n1. Always test your code change unless the change is not testable.\n\n2. If the change is not testable, highlight the reason why it's not testable to get the user's attention.\n\n3. By default, do end-to-end testing on everything that may potentially be impacted by your change on the local dev environment.\n\n4. Search for how the dev environment should be started if it's not obvious.\n\n5. Look for README files in the project for related info.\n\n6. Check if the local dev is already running before you try to start it again.\n\n## Software Engineering Principles\n\n1. **Don't reinvent the wheel (avoid Not Invented Here).** Before building anything, check whether the codebase or the libraries it already uses do it, and reuse that. Re-implementing something is a decision that needs a stated justification.\n\n## Bug Fix Policy\n\n**Never apply a fix for a bug you cannot reproduce.**\n\nBefore writing any fix:\n1. Reproduce the bug first in the local dev environment.\n2. If the bug cannot be reproduced locally (e.g., only manifests in release mode, requires specific device conditions, or the debug framework masks the crash), stop and tell the user — do not apply the fix.\n3. A fix is only valid if you can demonstrate the broken behavior before the fix and the correct behavior after.\n\n## Epistemic Discipline — Root Cause of Repeated Errors\n\n**The core failure mode: confusing \"generating a plausible explanation\" with \"knowing the answer.\"**\n\nCoherent ≠ true. A narrative that feels right because it's internally consistent is not a verified fact.\n\nRules to follow every time:\n\n1. **Search before asserting, not after being challenged.** The internet is always available. Use it proactively, not reactively as damage control.\n\n2. **Explicitly label inference vs. verified fact.** If it hasn't been confirmed by a source, say \"my hypothesis is...\" or \"I'd expect...\" — never state it as fact.\n\n3. **\"I don't know yet\" is a complete and correct answer.** Saying it is more helpful than fabricating a confident-sounding explanation.\n\n## Problem Definition Before Search\n\n**A well-defined problem statement is more valuable than a clever hypothesis.**\n\nThe discipline:\n1. **Define the problem precisely first.** Strip out assumptions one by one until the statement contains only what is actually known. Remove irrelevant context (e.g. subscription type if it doesn't affect the symptom).\n2. **Search for the problem directly** — not for your hypothesis. People who already solved the same problem will surface the real root cause.\n3. **Let the search results form the conclusion.** Don't search to confirm what you already believe.\n4. **Be flexible about scope.** Start with the most likely affected area (e.g. Discord) but stay open to other channels or systems being equally or more affected.\n\nSearching to validate a hypothesis finds confirmation of whatever you already believed. Searching for the problem finds answers.\n\n\n## GCP / kubectl Credential Policy\n\nAlways use the service account `ai-agent@tsdtechnology.iam.gserviceaccount.com` for all `gcloud` and `kubectl` operations. Before running any such command, switch to it:\n\n```bash\ngcloud config set account ai-agent@tsdtechnology.iam.gserviceaccount.com\n```\n\nThe personal account `k@obico.io` is reserved for the user's own interactive CLI use and its OAuth tokens expire — do not rely on it.\n\n## Sentry Access\n\n- Instance: `sentry.obico.io`\n- API base: `https://sentry.obico.io/api/0/`\n- Auth token: `REDACTED`\n- Usage: `curl -H \"Authorization: Bearer <token>\" <api_url>`"},{"path":"/Users/kenneth/Projects/JusPrin/.claude/worktrees/first-run-flow-f7be35/CLAUDE.md","type":"Project","content":"@AGENTS.md"},{"path":"/Users/kenneth/Projects/JusPrin/.claude/worktrees/first-run-flow-f7be35/AGENTS.md","type":"Project","content":"# CLAUDE.md\n\nJusPrin — a long-lived fork of OrcaSlicer (open-source C++17 3D slicer, wxWidgets GUI, CMake build system).\n\n## Upstream vs. fork attribution\n\nThis checkout holds two codebases: upstream OrcaSlicer (the `upstream` remote, SoftFever/OrcaSlicer) and the JusPrin fork's changes on top of it. Fork changes include everything under `src/slic3r/GUI/JusPrin/` and the seams the fork added inside OrcaSlicer-owned files.\n\n- Say \"OrcaSlicer does X\" only for behavior upstream ships. Behavior added by a fork commit is \"JusPrin does X\", even when it lives in an OrcaSlicer-owned file.\n- Check before attributing; the file path is not enough. Run `git blame` on the line, then `git branch -r --contains <sha> 'upstream/*'`. No upstream branch containing the commit means the fork owns it. Run `git fetch upstream` first if the remote may be stale.\n- If you have not checked, say it is unverified whether the behavior is upstream or fork.\n\n## Error handling discipline\n\n- Do not catch an error merely because it can be caught. Catch only when the code can recover, translate the error at an abstraction boundary, or add context that is essential for diagnosis. Logging alone is not recovery.\n- Represent expected states with normal control flow, not exceptions.\n- Never turn an invariant violation or an \"impossible\" state into success or a silent no-op. Fix the invalid transition at the layer that owns the decision and let unexpected failures propagate to Sentry.\n- Avoid duplicating defensive checks across layers. Keep lower-level operations strict, with their preconditions enforced by the caller that owns the workflow.\n- If concurrency makes an invalid state reachable, fix the ordering or synchronization instead of suppressing the resulting error.\n- Suppress an error only when it is genuinely expected and safely recoverable; document the reason and cover it with a test. Never broadly swallow unexpected errors.\n- Prefer removing the root cause over reducing the visibility of its symptoms. Tests should encode valid transitions and assert that invalid ones remain visible.\n\n## Build Commands\n\n```bash\n# macOS\ncmake --build build/arm64 --config RelWithDebInfo --target all --\n\n# Linux\ncmake --build build --config RelWithDebInfo --target all --\n\n# Windows (replace %build_type% with Debug/Release/RelWithDebInfo)\ncmake --build . --config %build_type% --target ALL_BUILD -- -m\n```\n\nNode/npm is required to build the Agent and Home pages, or configure with `-DJUSPRIN_BUILD_WEB=OFF` and supply the bundles.\n\n### Building on a shared machine\n\nSeveral agent sessions build on the same Mac at once. Without a limit, ninja runs one compile job per core plus two, so every build you start can take the whole machine. In 2026-10 one session stacked four builds in one build directory: 56 clang processes on 12 cores. It kept restarting its build to change targets and never limited the job count.\n\n- Before starting a build, check `uptime` and `pgrep -fl ninja`. If the load is already above the core count, wait or build less.\n- Limit jobs: `cmake --build … -- -j4`.\n- Decide the full target list before starting. Never restart a running build to add or change targets: let it finish, then build the rest.\n- Run one build per build directory. Two ninja processes in one directory compete and can leave its records inconsistent.\n- Stopping a build means stopping ninja. `pkill -f \"cmake --build …\"` kills only the cmake wrapper, and ninja keeps compiling. Start long builds in their own process group (`perl -e 'setpgrp(0,0); exec @ARGV' cmake --build …`) and stop them with `kill -- -<pgid>`. Then confirm with `pgrep -fl ninja` before building again.\n- When a build is slow, count your own compile processes before blaming the load on other sessions.\n- Reuse an existing build where the task allows. Ask before configuring a fresh full-app build the task does not require.\n\n## Testing\n\nCatch2 framework. Tests in `tests/` directory.\n\n```bash\ncd build && ctest --output-on-failure           # all tests\nctest --test-dir ./tests/libslic3r              # individual suite\nctest --test-dir ./tests/fff_print\n```\n\nPrinter UI without hardware: `src/slic3r/GUI/JusPrin/Testing/README.md` (Bambu in-process fake); Moonraker and OctoPrint: `tests/printer_hosts/README.md` (Docker). Isolated `--datadir`; do not drive another JusPrin instance.\n\n## Code Style\n\n- C++17, selective C++20. PascalCase classes, snake_case functions/variables\n- `#pragma once` for headers. Smart pointers and RAII preferred\n- Parallelization via TBB — be mindful of shared state\n- Wording: a file or object is \"opened\", \"imported\", \"loaded\" or \"added\", never \"arrived\". \"Arrive\" is fine for messages, responses, events and lists. `AgentUI/src/wording.test.ts` guards the text the model reads.\n- Wording the model reads calls the product \"the app\", never Orca or OrcaSlicer: the model speaks as the app, and a second name reads as a second program. `AgentUI/src/wording.test.ts` guards this too. Log lines may name Orca. A throw may only if it never reaches the model: tool results forward save and open errors as they are, and the guard does not see those strings.\n\n## Key Entry Points\n\n- App startup: `src/OrcaSlicer.cpp`\n- Slicing pipeline: `src/libslic3r/Print.cpp`\n- All print/printer/material settings: `src/libslic3r/PrintConfig.cpp`\n- GUI: `src/slic3r/GUI/`\n- Core algorithms: `src/libslic3r/` (GCode/, Fill/, Support/, Geometry/, Format/, Arachne/)\n- Printer profiles: `resources/profiles/[manufacturer].json`\n\n## JusPrin Production UI\n\n- Start with `agent-docs/jusprin/README.md` for the canonical product definition, production architecture, verification method, Orca integration guidance, and design system.\n- Before editing any file outside `src/slic3r/GUI/JusPrin/`, read `agent-docs/jusprin/fork-stewardship.md`. Every line changed in an OrcaSlicer-owned file is a line this fork re-resolves at every rebase, so those changes must be small, product-neutral, additive where possible, and accompanied by the rebase evidence that document requires.\n- Experimental plans, probes, screenshots, logs, and temporary UI code remain on `jusprin-v2-poc`. Use `agent-docs/jusprin/poc-reference.md` for exact commit and path pointers; do not cherry-pick the POC wholesale.\n- Curated brand and semantic UI assets live under `resources/jusprin/`.\n- Before writing or changing any UI code, native or Agent page, follow \"Writing UI code\" in `agent-docs/jusprin/design-system.md`: every color, radius, font, and control size comes from the token file through `ShellTheme`, the shell recipes, or the CSS variables. A value with no token gets a token and a guard first, never a literal.\n- Temporary working docs (handoffs, findings, plans written for another agent) stay untracked: never commit them, and never add them to `.gitignore` or `.git/info/exclude`. Untracked files in `git status` are how Kenneth tracks work that isn't finished.\n\n## Landing a branch on jusprin-newui\n\n- Merge a feature branch with `git merge --no-ff`, never a squash. Make the merge message one line saying what the branch delivers: `Merge claude/<branch>: <what it delivers>`.\n- Read the history one line per feature with `git log --first-parent jusprin-newui`.\n- Why: a squash drops the step commits that `git blame` and `git bisect` need. It also leaves every branch the squashed one had merged looking unmerged, so the same work gets merged again with conflicts. Kenneth chose this on 2026-11-05.\n\n## Critical Constraints\n\n- **Backward compatibility required** for .3mf project files and printer profiles\n- **Cross-platform** — all changes must work on Windows, macOS, and Linux\n- Profile/format changes need version migration handling\n- Dependencies built separately in `deps/build/`, then linked to main app\n\n## Code review focus areas\n\n- Changes must not cause regressions in existing functionality, defaults, profiles, or project compatibility.\n- Features gated by options must not affect existing behavior when those options are disabled.\n- Changes should follow the existing code style and architecture. Architectural changes should be justified in code comments and the PR description.\n- Add helper functions or utilities only when existing code cannot reasonably be reused. Avoid duplication.\n- Changes to OrcaSlicer-owned files must be additive where possible. Rewriting an upstream function, or reimplementing upstream behavior beside it, needs explicit justification in the PR description.\n- When an upstream line is deleted or relocated, say where it went and what a future rebase must carry into the new home.\n- Keep code concise and clear. Manually simplify AI generated bloated codes before review.\n- Include targeted tests or documented verification for behavior changes, especially in slicing logic, profiles, formats, and GUI defaults."},{"path":"/Users/kenneth/.claude/projects/-Users-kenneth-Projects-JusPrin/memory/MEMORY.md","type":"AutoMem","content":"- [Before running or concluding](evidence-before-handing-kenneth-a-decision.md) — write the question and who…\n- [Guess is not a finding](guessed-cause-is-not-a-finding.md) — name what would disprove a cause and run it; label \"u