{"parentUuid":"726ab25b-5c13-48cb-86fc-c7a6c57cfe03","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgkfPSqKE3pR5xCAc1jTW","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Ji2VF5mHAp4jthqeoijybz","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd ~/.claude/projects/-Users-kenneth-Projects-JusPrin--claude-worktrees-first-run-flow-f7be35 && python3 - <<'EOF'\nimport json\nfor line in open('c868826d-3950-4f0f-9ecd-4a549cdd6983.jsonl'):\n    d=json.loads(line)\n    if d.get('type')!='assistant': continue\n    for c in d['message'].get('content',[]):\n        if c.get('type')=='text' and 'datadir' in c['text']:\n            print(d.get('timestamp'), c['text'][:1500]); print('----')\nEOF"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1772,"cache_read_input_tokens":117397,"output_tokens":328,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":73},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1772,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":328,"cache_read_input_tokens":117397,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1772,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1772},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01Ji2VF5mHAp4jthqeoijybz":{"command":"cd ~/.claude/projects/-Users-kenneth-Projects-JusPrin--claude-worktrees-first-run-flow-f7be35 && python3 - <<'EOF'\nimport json\nfor line in open('c868826d-3950-4f0f-9ecd-4a549cdd6983.jsonl'):\n    d=json.loads(line)\n    if d.get('type')!='assistant': continue\n    for c in d['message'].get('content',[]):\n        if c.get('type')=='text' and 'datadir' in c['text']:\n            print(d.get('timestamp'), c['text'][:1500]); print('----')\nEOF"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CgkfPScuYUi9VmWDbV9Fb","requestRef":"de95cfba-a6fa-467e-87a0-2a03394fe110","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"8a318f44-aa3a-44d1-86d7-c3295ffb2276","timestamp":"2026-11-05T23:38:53.998Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"high","perTurnEffort":"high","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/kenneth/Projects/JusPrin/.claude/worktrees/first-run-flow-f7be35","sessionId":"c868826d-3950-4f0f-9ecd-4a549cdd6983","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"claude/first-run-printer-step","slug":"dapper-knitting-moler"}
{"parentUuid":"8a318f44-aa3a-44d1-86d7-c3295ffb2276","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"d97ef8e1-febe-4fa1-82dd-c20e66105c0f","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Ji2VF5mHAp4jthqeoijybz","type":"tool_result","content":"2026-11-03T17:14:05.207Z No, I wasn't hunting for a gap you would find. I had already tested both sign-in paths before you asked. The problem was that I had deleted the local server's data during clean-up, and the setup existed only as one-off commands I had typed during the session. So there was no command I could hand you that would work as-is. I wrote a script and ran it from an empty database, which passed. Then I started also checking its password helper, which went further than you asked for. I should have handed you the commands at that point and said which part was untested.\n\nThe local server is running now, already set up. Here are the commands.\n\n**1. Start the local server.** It is already up, and running this again is safe. From scratch it takes about 2.5 minutes.\n\n```bash\ncd ~/Projects/tsd-enterprise-worktrees/email-sign-in && ./email-sign-in-local.sh up\n```\n\n**2. Make an empty data folder that points the app at the local server:**\n\n```bash\nD=~/jusprin-signin-test; rm -rf $D; mkdir -p $D; echo '{\"jusprin_server\": {\"base_url\": \"http://localhost:3534\"}}' > $D/JusPrin2.conf\n```\n\n**3. Launch the built app on that folder:**\n\n```bash\n/Users/kenneth/Projects/JusPrin/.claude/worktrees/first-run-flow-f7be35/build/arm64/src/Release/OrcaSlicer.app/Contents/MacOS/OrcaSlicer --datadir ~/jusprin-signin-test\n```\n\nFor dark mode, add `-AppleInterfaceStyle Dark` at the end. I haven't run the app that way.\n\n**4. While you click through, from the email-sign-in folder:**\n\nPrint the last code the server emaile\n----\n2026-11-03T17:20:51.113Z Nothing about how the server works or is deployed changes. Step 2 is not for the server at all. It is for the JusPrin desktop app.\n\n**Step 2 (the app's data folder).** It makes the app start like a brand-new install and talk to your local server instead of production.\n- `--datadir ~/jusprin-signin-test` tells the app to keep its settings in that folder instead of your real one in `~/Library/Application Support/JusPrin2`. You see the first-launch welcome, and your real JusPrin settings and account are never touched.\n- The one line in `JusPrin2.conf` is the app setting `jusprin_server.base_url`. It points the app at `http://localhost:3534` instead of the production server.\n\n**Step 1 (the local server).** This is the same kind of local Docker dev stack as your other local stacks. The script does four routine things:\n- It starts the containers with `docker compose up`.\n- It runs Django's `migrate` on the empty local database.\n- It adds two rows to that local database:\n  - the Site `localhost:3534`, so the server knows which site requests come from;\n  - the OAuth client `JusPrin2`, which is the app's ID.\n- It sends email to the container log, so you can read the codes there.\n\nI'd expect production to already have both rows, but I haven't checked that. Nothing here is deployed, pushed, or touches production. The compose file and the script are untracked local files in the tsd-enterprise email-sign-in worktree.\n\n**The real server code change** is the one the brief asked for: commit \n----\n2026-11-03T22:49:13.212Z Here are the commands. They run the email sign-in code from the claude/email-sign-in worktree as a separate test server on port 3536, with its own database. Your normal local server on port 3334 and its database stay as they are. That matters because the normal database is missing the branch's migrations and the JusPrin2 app. Your normal local stack must be running, since the test server uses its database and Redis containers.\n\nI ran steps 1 and 2 myself just now and then removed everything. Asking for a code reached the code step, the code showed up in the server's log, and a password login returned a token.\n\n**1. Start the test server.** The first start takes a few minutes while it installs packages and creates the tables.\n\n```bash\nW=/Users/kenneth/Projects/tsd-enterprise-worktrees/email-sign-in; E=$(mktemp); docker exec tsd-enterprise-db-1 createdb -U tsd-dev tsd-dev-email-e2e; docker inspect tsd-enterprise-web-1 --format '{{range .Config.Env}}{{println .}}{{end}}' | grep -v '^$' | sed -E 's#^(DATABASE_URL=.*/)tsd-dev$#\\1tsd-dev-email-e2e#; s#^REDIS_URL=.*#REDIS_URL=redis://redis:6379/9#' > $E; docker run -d --name email-e2e-web --network tsd-enterprise_default --env-file $E -e SENTRY_DSN= -e EMAIL_BACKEND=django.core.mail.backends.console.EmailBackend -p 3536:3536 -v $W/obico-server/backend:/app -v $W/web_ent:/web_ent -v /Users/kenneth/Projects/tsd-enterprise/web_ent/frontend:/web_ent/frontend:ro -w /web_ent tsd-enterprise-web sh -c 'pip install -q -r /app/requirements.tx\n----\n2026-11-03T23:09:30.383Z The email sign-in now runs on your normal local server: the usual Docker Compose stack in `/Users/kenneth/Projects/tsd-enterprise`, on port 3334. There is no second container, port or database anymore. I deleted the scripts that started one, and saved the rule in memory so later sessions follow it.\n\n**What I changed on your machine:**\n- **Main checkout.** It now has the email sign-in commit checked out (detached), instead of `credits-integration-2026-11`. Nothing is lost: that branch is fully contained in the email sign-in branch. Your untracked files are untouched.\n- **obico-server.** In the main checkout it's now at `origin/master` (the allauth 65 commit), plus the uncommitted change that looks addresses up per syndicate. That change now exists in two places: here and in the email sign-in worktree's obico-server. You only need to commit it once.\n- **Web and tasks containers.** Rebuilt, so they run allauth 65.\n- **Database.** Ran the 20 pending migrations on your local database, as you approved, and created the JusPrin2 sign-in app in it.\n\n**What I tested.**\n- Inside your web container, the 19 sign-in tests pass, and so do all 178 tests in the `jusprin` and `app` suites.\n- I opened the built app against port 3334. \"Continue with email\" sent nothing and showed the choice. A wrong password showed \"That password is wrong.\"\n- I didn't send a code: your local server sends real email through Amazon SES. So use an address you can read, never `@example.com`.\n\n**Commands to test it y\n----\n2026-11-03T23:18:40.777Z Both branches are pushed, and GitHub matches local: JusPrin's `credits-integration-2026-11` is at ae1b916bbe and tsd-enterprise's at 17e87ee16e.\n\nStart the server:\n\n```bash\ncd /Users/kenneth/Projects/tsd-enterprise && docker compose up -d\n```\n\nMake a fresh data folder pointed at that server and open the app with it:\n\n```bash\nD=$(mktemp -d) && echo '{\"jusprin_server\":{\"base_url\":\"http://localhost:3334\"}}' > $D/JusPrin2.conf && /Users/kenneth/Projects/JusPrin/.claude/worktrees/first-run-flow-f7be35/build/arm64/src/Release/OrcaSlicer.app/Contents/MacOS/OrcaSlicer --datadir $D\n```\n\nEach run of the second command starts from an empty data folder, so you get the first launch every time.\n\nThese two commands work as-is right now, because I already rebuilt the containers, ran the migrations and created the JusPrin2 app in your database. After you pull server changes that add Python packages or migrations, you'll need to build and migrate once more. The app is the build in the first-run worktree, and it's built from the commit you just pushed.\n\nThe local server sends real email, so for the code path use an address you can read.\n----\n2026-11-04T00:06:35.043Z This command opens JusPrin on a new, empty data folder pointed at staging (https://app-stg.obico.io), so you get the first launch every time:\n\n```bash\nD=$(mktemp -d) && echo '{\"jusprin_server\":{\"base_url\":\"https://app-stg.obico.io\"}}' > $D/JusPrin2.conf && /Users/kenneth/Projects/JusPrin/.claude/worktrees/first-run-flow-f7be35/build/arm64/src/Release/OrcaSlicer.app/Contents/MacOS/OrcaSlicer --datadir $D\n```\n\nIt's the same command as the local one, with the server address changed from `http://localhost:3334` to staging. You don't need the local server running for this.\n\nI launched it once with a throwaway folder. The app started and still had the staging address in its settings file afterwards. It doesn't contact the server until you sign in, so I haven't seen it reach staging, and I didn't sign in.\n\nStaging sends real email, so the code arrives in the inbox of whatever address you enter.\n----\n2026-11-04T00:45:03.189Z Staging has been fixed in another session you started, so Google sign-in on staging should finish now. That session added your OpenRouter key to staging and applied the credits config, and I checked its work:\n\n- Staging's model setting now lists two models: Standard at 1 credit a message and Expert at 5 credits a message.\n- The staging secret now has `JUSPRIN_OPENROUTER_API_KEY` next to the Anthropic and OpenAI keys.\n- The web and websocket deployments are running and still on image obi.4060.\n- The key file in `/tmp` has been deleted.\n- The change is recorded in the ops repo as commit 19208be, locally only.\n\nNobody has tried a real sign-in from the app yet. To test it, run the same command as before; each run starts from a new, empty data folder:\n\n```bash\nD=$(mktemp -d) && echo '{\"jusprin_server\":{\"base_url\":\"https://app-stg.obico.io\"}}' > $D/JusPrin2.conf && /Users/kenneth/Projects/JusPrin/.claude/worktrees/first-run-flow-f7be35/build/arm64/src/Release/OrcaSlicer.app/Contents/MacOS/OrcaSlicer --datadir $D\n```\n\nProduction still lacks the credits config. It's fine today, because production runs obi.4055, which predates the ledger. Whoever releases the ledger code to production has to apply the production half of `claude/credits-gateway-config` and add the OpenRouter key to production's secret first. Otherwise production gets the same broken model list.\n----\n2026-11-05T20:24:08.227Z Not everything is done. The server changes are pushed, but the app change and the staging deployment records are still only on this Mac. Production hasn't been rolled out at all.\n\n**Where each piece is:**\n\n- **JusPrin app:** branch `claude/elated-sutherland-84a741`, ending at 4b16e4b1eb. It's 19 commits ahead of `origin/jusprin-newui` and isn't pushed. The syndicate session asked the Moonraker test session to squash and merge it, but that hasn't happened: `jusprin-newui` doesn't contain it, locally or on GitHub.\n- **tsd-enterprise:** pushed. The syndicate work is one squashed commit, 1845fe74b9, on `origin/master` and on `origin/release`, favicon included. The unsquashed history is still on the local branch `claude/jusprin-syndicate`.\n- **obico-server:** pushed. JusPrin 2's own light theme and colors are f78ef6e8 on `origin/master`.\n- **ops:** not pushed. The local branch `claude/jusprin-host` has 8 commits: the jusprinv2 host, ingress and certificate, the syndicate set-up, and the staging releases obi.4072 and obi.4073. Staging already runs those changes, because they were applied with kubectl, but GitHub has no record of them.\n- **Production:** jusprinv2.obico.io doesn't respond yet. The Google settings, DNS and app record for it still need to be set up.\n\n**Testing against staging.** The build in the syndicate session's folder is current with its branch. This command starts it against staging with an empty data folder, so you see the first launch:\n\n```bash\nJUSPRIN_AGENT_END\n----\n2026-11-05T22:13:52.651Z This runs the app from the main checkout, which I built at 12:42 from the latest jusprin-newui. It points the app at staging and starts it with an empty data folder, so you get the first launch from the Welcome screen:\n\n```bash\nrm -rf ~/JusPrinStagingTest && JUSPRIN_AGENT_ENDPOINT=https://jusprinv2-stg.obico.io/ent/jusprin/v2/responses /Users/kenneth/Projects/JusPrin/build/arm64/src/RelWithDebInfo/OrcaSlicer.app/Contents/MacOS/OrcaSlicer --datadir ~/JusPrinStagingTest\n```\n\nThe `rm -rf` clears the previous test run's data folder, so each launch starts the first-run flow from scratch. Right now that folder doesn't exist, and no other copy of the app is running.\n\nA few things to know:\n\n- Sign-in goes to jusprinv2-stg.obico.io in your system browser. Staging sends real emails, so for email sign-in use a `k+something@obico.io` address.\n- The app's Terminal output shows in the window you ran it from. Closing that terminal closes the app.\n- I've confirmed this command starts the app, but I haven't clicked through the first launch on this build myself.\n----","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"b82b82c8-729a-48f7-9a2a-def27ddc8a53","timestamp":"2026-11-05T23:38:54.364Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"2026-11-03T17:14:05.207Z No, I wasn't hunting for a gap you would find. I had already tested both sign-in paths before you asked. The problem was that I had deleted the local server's data during clean-up, and the setup existed only as one-off commands I had typed during the session. So there was no command I could hand you that would work as-is. I wrote a script and ran it from an empty database, which passed. Then I started also checking its password helper, which went further than you asked for. I should have handed you the commands at that point and said which part was untested.\n\nThe local server is running now, already set up. Here are the commands.\n\n**1. Start the local server.** It is already up, and running this again is safe. From scratch it takes about 2.5 minutes.\n\n```bash\ncd ~/Projects/tsd-enterprise-worktrees/email-sign-in && ./email-sign-in-local.sh up\n```\n\n**2. Make an empty data folder that points the app at the local server:**\n\n```bash\nD=~/jusprin-signin-test; rm -rf $D; mkdir -p $D; echo '{\"jusprin_server\": {\"base_url\": \"http://localhost:3534\"}}' > $D/JusPrin2.conf\n```\n\n**3. Launch the built app on that folder:**\n\n```bash\n/Users/kenneth/Projects/JusPrin/.claude/worktrees/first-run-flow-f7be35/build/arm64/src/Release/OrcaSlicer.app/Contents/MacOS/OrcaSlicer --datadir ~/jusprin-signin-test\n```\n\nFor dark mode, add `-AppleInterfaceStyle Dark` at the end. I haven't run the app that way.\n\n**4. While you click through, from the email-sign-in folder:**\n\nPrint the last code the server emaile\n----\n2026-11-03T17:20:51.113Z Nothing about how the server works or is deployed changes. Step 2 is not for the server at all. It is for the JusPrin desktop app.\n\n**Step 2 (the app's data folder).** It makes the app start like a brand-new install and talk to your local server instead of production.\n- `--datadir ~/jusprin-signin-test` tells the app to keep its settings in that folder instead of your real one in `~/Library/Application Support/JusPrin2`. You see the first-launch welcome, and your real JusPrin settings and account are never touched.\n- The one line in `JusPrin2.conf` is the app setting `jusprin_server.base_url`. It points the app at `http://localhost:3534` instead of the production server.\n\n**Step 1 (the local server).** This is the same kind of local Docker dev stack as your other local stacks. The script does four routine things:\n- It starts the containers with `docker compose up`.\n- It runs Django's `migrate` on the empty local database.\n- It adds two rows to that local database:\n  - the Site `localhost:3534`, so the server knows which site requests come from;\n  - the OAuth client `JusPrin2`, which is the app's ID.\n- It sends email to the container log, so you can read the codes there.\n\nI'd expect production to already have both rows, but I haven't checked that. Nothing here is deployed, pushed, or touches production. The compose file and the script are untracked local files in the tsd-enterprise email-sign-in worktree.\n\n**The real server code change** is the one the brief asked for: commit \n----\n2026-11-03T22:49:13.212Z Here are the commands. They run the email sign-in code from the claude/email-sign-in worktree as a separate test server on port 3536, with its own database. Your normal local server on port 3334 and its database stay as they are. That matters because the normal database is missing the branch's migrations and the JusPrin2 app. Your normal local stack must be running, since the test server uses its database and Redis containers.\n\nI ran steps 1 and 2 myself just now and then removed everything. Asking for a code reached the code step, the code showed up in the server's log, and a password login returned a token.\n\n**1. Start the test server.** The first start takes a few minutes while it installs packages and creates the tables.\n\n```bash\nW=/Users/kenneth/Projects/tsd-enterprise-worktrees/email-sign-in; E=$(mktemp); docker exec tsd-enterprise-db-1 createdb -U tsd-dev tsd-dev-email-e2e; docker inspect tsd-enterprise-web-1 --format '{{range .Config.Env}}{{println .}}{{end}}' | grep -v '^$' | sed -E 's#^(DATABASE_URL=.*/)tsd-dev$#\\1tsd-dev-email-e2e#; s#^REDIS_URL=.*#REDIS_URL=redis://redis:6379/9#' > $E; docker run -d --name email-e2e-web --network tsd-enterprise_default --env-file $E -e SENTRY_DSN= -e EMAIL_BACKEND=django.core.mail.backends.console.EmailBackend -p 3536:3536 -v $W/obico-server/backend:/app -v $W/web_ent:/web_ent -v /Users/kenneth/Projects/tsd-enterprise/web_ent/frontend:/web_ent/frontend:ro -w /web_ent tsd-enterprise-web sh -c 'pip install -q -r /app/requirements.tx\n----\n2026-11-03T23:09:30.383Z The email sign-in now runs on your normal local server: the usual Docker Compose stack in `/Users/kenneth/Projects/tsd-enterprise`, on port 3334. There is no second container, port or database anymore. I deleted the scripts that started one, and saved the rule in memory so later sessions follow it.\n\n**What I changed on your machine:**\n- **Main checkout.** It now has the email sign-in commit checked out (detached), instead of `credits-integration-2026-11`. Nothing is lost: that branch is fully contained in the email sign-in branch. Your untracked files are untouched.\n- **obico-server.** In the main checkout it's now at `origin/master` (the allauth 65 commit), plus the uncommitted change that looks addresses up per syndicate. That change now exists in two places: here and in the email sign-in worktree's obico-server. You only need to commit it once.\n- **Web and tasks containers.** Rebuilt, so they run allauth 65.\n- **Database.** Ran the 20 pending migrations on your local database, as you approved, and created the JusPrin2 sign-in app in it.\n\n**What I tested.**\n- Inside your web container, the 19 sign-in tests pass, and so do all 178 tests in the `jusprin` and `app` suites.\n- I opened the built app against port 3334. \"Continue with email\" sent nothing and showed the choice. A wrong password showed \"That password is wrong.\"\n- I didn't send a code: your local server sends real email through Amazon SES. So use an address you can read, never `@example.com`.\n\n**Commands to test it y\n----\n2026-11-03T23:18:40.777Z Both branches are pushed, and GitHub matches local: JusPrin's `credits-integration-2026-11` is at ae1b916bbe and tsd-enterprise's at 17e87ee16e.\n\nStart the server:\n\n```bash\ncd /Users/kenneth/Projects/tsd-enterprise && docker compose up -d\n```\n\nMake a fresh data folder pointed at that server and open the app with it:\n\n```bash\nD=$(mktemp -d) && echo '{\"jusprin_server\":{\"base_url\":\"http://localhost:3334\"}}' > $D/JusPrin2.conf && /Users/kenneth/Projects/JusPrin/.claude/worktrees/first-run-flow-f7be35/build/arm64/src/Release/OrcaSlicer.app/Contents/MacOS/OrcaSlicer --datadir $D\n```\n\nEach run of the second command starts from an empty data folder, so you get the first launch every time.\n\nThese two commands work as-is right now, because I already rebuilt the containers, ran the migrations and created the JusPrin2 app in your database. After you pull server changes that add Python packages or migrations, you'll need to build and migrate once more. The app is the build in the first-run worktree, and it's built from the commit you just pushed.\n\nThe local server sends real email, so for the code path use an address you can read.\n----\n2026-11-04T00:06:35.043Z This command opens JusPrin on a new, empty data folder pointed at staging (https://app-stg.obico.io), so you get the first launch every time:\n\n```bash\nD=$(mktemp -d) && echo '{\"jusprin_server\":{\"base_url\":\"https://app-stg.obico.io\"}}' > $D/JusPrin2.conf && /Users/kenneth/Projects/JusPrin/.claude/worktrees/first-run-flow-f7be35/build/arm64/src/Release/OrcaSlicer.app/Contents/MacOS/OrcaSlicer --datadir $D\n```\n\nIt's the same command as the local one, with the server address changed from `http://localhost:3334` to staging. You don't need the local server running for this.\n\nI launched it once with a throwaway folder. The app started and still had the staging address in its settings file afterwards. It doesn't contact the server until you sign in, so I haven't seen it reach staging, and I didn't sign in.\n\nStaging sends real email, so the code arrives in the inbox of whatever address you enter.\n----\n2026-11-04T00:45:03.189Z Staging has been fixed in another session you started, so Google sign-in on staging should finish now. That session added your OpenRouter key to staging and applied the credits config, and I checked its work:\n\n- Staging's model setting now lists two models: Standard at 1 credit a message and Expert at 5 credits a message.\n- The staging secret now has `JUSPRIN_OPENROUTER_API_KEY` next to the Anthropic and OpenAI keys.\n- The web and websocket deployments are running and still on image obi.4060.\n- The key file in `/tmp` has been deleted.\n- The change is recorded in the ops repo as commit 19208be, locally only.\n\nNobody has tried a real sign-in from the app yet. To test it, run the same command as before; each run starts from a new, empty data folder:\n\n```bash\nD=$(mktemp -d) && echo '{\"jusprin_server\":{\"base_url\":\"https://app-stg.obico.io\"}}' > $D/JusPrin2.conf && /Users/kenneth/Projects/JusPrin/.claude/worktrees/first-run-flow-f7be35/build/arm64/src/Release/OrcaSlicer.app/Contents/MacOS/OrcaSlicer --datadir $D\n```\n\nProduction still lacks the credits config. It's fine today, because production runs obi.4055, which predates the ledger. Whoever releases the ledger code to production has to apply the production half of `claude/credits-gateway-config` and add the OpenRouter key to production's secret first. Otherwise production gets the same broken model list.\n----\n2026-11-05T20:24:08.227Z Not everything is done. The server changes are pushed, but the app change and the staging deployment records are still only on this Mac. Production hasn't been rolled out at all.\n\n**Where each piece is:**\n\n- **JusPrin app:** branch `claude/elated-sutherland-84a741`, ending at 4b16e4b1eb. It's 19 commits ahead of `origin/jusprin-newui` and isn't pushed. The syndicate session asked the Moonraker test session to squash and merge it, but that hasn't happened: `jusprin-newui` doesn't contain it, locally or on GitHub.\n- **tsd-enterprise:** pushed. The syndicate work is one squashed commit, 1845fe74b9, on `origin/master` and on `origin/release`, favicon included. The unsquashed history is still on the local branch `claude/jusprin-syndicate`.\n- **obico-server:** pushed. JusPrin 2's own light theme and colors are f78ef6e8 on `origin/master`.\n- **ops:** not pushed. The local branch `claude/jusprin-host` has 8 commits: the jusprinv2 host, ingress and certificate, the syndicate set-up, and the staging releases obi.4072 and obi.4073. Staging already runs those changes, because they were applied with kubectl, but GitHub has no record of them.\n- **Production:** jusprinv2.obico.io doesn't respond yet. The Google settings, DNS and app record for it still need to be set up.\n\n**Testing against staging.** The build in the syndicate session's folder is current with its branch. This command starts it against staging with an empty data folder, so you see the first launch:\n\n```bash\nJUSPRIN_AGENT_END\n----\n2026-11-05T22:13:52.651Z This runs the app from the main checkout, which I built at 12:42 from the latest jusprin-newui. It points the app at staging and starts it with an empty data folder, so you get the first launch from the Welcome screen:\n\n```bash\nrm -rf ~/JusPrinStagingTest && JUSPRIN_AGENT_ENDPOINT=https://jusprinv2-stg.obico.io/ent/jusprin/v2/responses /Users/kenneth/Projects/JusPrin/build/arm64/src/RelWithDebInfo/OrcaSlicer.app/Contents/MacOS/OrcaSlicer --datadir ~/JusPrinStagingTest\n```\n\nThe `rm -rf` clears the previous test run's data folder, so each launch starts the first-run flow from scratch. Right now that folder doesn't exist, and no other copy of the app is running.\n\nA few things to know:\n\n- Sign-in goes to jusprinv2-stg.obico.io in your system browser. Staging sends real emails, so for email sign-in use a `k+something@obico.io` address.\n- The app's Terminal output shows in the window you ran it from. Closing that terminal closes the app.\n- I've confirmed this command starts the app, but I haven't clicked through the first launch on this build myself.\n----","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sandboxed":false,"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"8a318f44-aa3a-44d1-86d7-c3295ffb2276","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/kenneth/.claude/projects/-Users-kenneth-Projects-JusPrin--claude-worktrees-first-run-flow-f7be35","sessionId":"c868826d-3950-4f0f-9ecd-4a549cdd6983","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"claude/first-run-printer-step","slug":"dapper-knitting-moler"}
